\begin{abstract}
The N-Queens problem is a classic combinatorial constraint satisfaction problem.
This paper presents a comprehensive empirical comparison of SAT encoding methods
for N-Queens, evaluating four cardinality constraint encodings (binary, commander,
product, sequential counter) across four SAT solvers (CaDiCaL, Glucose, Kissat,
CryptoMiniSAT), and comparing against a CP-SAT baseline.
Experiments on board sizes N=8 to 16 show that sequential counter encoding with
Glucose4 achieves the fastest solve times, while binary encoding produces the
smallest CNF formulas. SAT solvers consistently outperform CP-SAT for this
problem class.
\end{abstract}

