\section{Discussion}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Sequential counter} encoding achieves fastest solve times but introduces many auxiliary variables.
    \item \textbf{Binary} encoding produces smallest formulas but scales poorly due to $O(k^2)$ clauses.
    \item \textbf{Glucose4} is the most consistent SAT solver across encodings.
    \item \textbf{SAT solvers} significantly outperform CP-SAT for N-Queens.
    \item CP-SAT time does not scale monotonically due to its different search strategy.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
For N-Queens, sequential counter encoding with Glucose4 provides the best
performance. SAT-based approaches are superior to CP-SAT for this problem class.

